.\" Manual page for the udevmon command. Keep it in step with the CLI:
.\" tests/test_manpages.py fails when a command or option is missing here.
.TH UDEVMON 1 "2026-11-10" "udevmon" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
udevmon \- run your own commands when USB devices and filesystems appear or go away
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B udevmon
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ]
.br
.B udevmon \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B udevmon
watches USB devices (through udev) and filesystem mounts (through udisks2 on
the D\-Bus system bus) and runs commands that you define when something
happens to them: a stick is plugged in, a filesystem is mounted, a drive is
pulled out without being ejected, and so on.
It runs entirely as a normal user and needs no root privileges.
It is one event\-driven process: no polling, and no CPU use when idle.
.PP
The configuration is a TOML file, described in
.BR udevmon.toml (5).
You edit it by hand or with the commands below, which keep your comments and
layout.
.PP
The concepts:
.TP
.B device
A name you give to one physical USB device or filesystem, with the properties
that identify it (for example a serial number or a filesystem UUID).
.TP
.B event
What happened:
.BR connected ,
.BR disconnected ,
.BR mounted ,
.BR unmounted ,
.BR removed ,
.B startup
or
.BR shutdown .
.TP
.B tag
A label that lets one action cover several devices.
A tag is either set on devices or selected by a rule on properties.
.TP
.B action
A command to run when an event happens to a device (or to anything with a
tag).
.PP
A typical session: run
.B udevmon scan
and plug something in to name it, add an action with
.BR "udevmon actions add" ,
try it with
.BR "udevmon run" ,
then install the background service with
.BR "udevmon service install \-\-now" .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options go before the command:
.B udevmon \-\-config my.toml run
(not after it).
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Use this configuration file instead of
.IR ~/.config/udevmon/config.toml .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Debug logging.
The daemon then logs why each event did or did not run an action.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
It is taken from git, in the format used by setuptools\-scm, for example
.BR 0.2.1.dev3+g1a2b3c4d5 .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help text.
Every command and sub\-command accepts it too.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "Running"
.TP
.B udevmon run \fR[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
Run the daemon in the foreground.
Only one daemon can run per user; a second one exits with
.RB \(lq "udevmon is already running" \(rq.
For things already present when it starts, it fires
.B startup
events; when it stops normally it fires
.B shutdown
events for what is still present, and waits for those actions to finish (up to
each action's timeout).
It starts with a line that shows the number of devices and actions, and
.B [PAUSED]
if automation is paused.
.IP
With
.B \-\-dry\-run
the daemon logs what it would run and runs nothing.
.IP
See
.B SIGNALS
for how to stop and reload it.
.TP
.B udevmon reload
Ask the running daemon to re\-read its configuration.
It exits with status 1 and the message
.RB \(lq "udevmon is not running" \(rq
if there is no daemon.
Reloading does not fire
.B startup
actions again; use
.B udevmon trigger
for a device configured while plugged in.
.TP
.B udevmon trigger \fIDEVICE\fR [\fIEVENT\fR] [\fB\-\-wait\fR]
Ask the running daemon to run the actions of the named device, which must be
present, for
.I EVENT
now:
.B startup
(the default),
.BR shutdown ,
.B connected
(a USB device),
.B mounted
(a filesystem that is mounted) or
.B unmounted
(a filesystem; it stays mounted).
It goes through the same rules, locks, timeouts and placeholders as a real event
and is logged like one (marked
.BR "udevmon trigger" ),
but it is never dropped by the debounce and removes no state folder.
It prints what it started and why any action was skipped, and exits with status
1 when the daemon is not running, the device is unknown to the daemon's
configuration, not present, ignored, or automation is paused.
.B \-\-wait
waits for the actions to finish and prints their exit status; then any status
other than 0 also gives status 1.
It talks to the daemon over its control socket,
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/udevmon.sock .
.TP
.B udevmon service install \fR[\fB\-\-now\fR] [\fB\-\-dest \fIDIR\fR]
Write a systemd user unit,
.IR ~/.config/systemd/user/udevmon.service .
The unit runs
.B udevmon run
(with your
.B \-\-config
if you gave one), restarts on failure, and is wanted by
.BR default.target ,
so it starts with your login session.
With
.B \-\-now
it also enables and starts the service.
With
.B \-\-dest
it only writes the unit file into that directory and never calls
.BR systemctl .
.TP
.B udevmon service uninstall \fR[\fB\-\-dest \fIDIR\fR]
Stop and disable the service (so its
.B shutdown
actions run), delete the unit file and reload systemd.
Only a file that looks like udevmon's own unit is deleted; anything else is
reported and left alone.
Safe to run again.
With
.B \-\-dest
only that file is removed and
.B systemctl
is not called.
Your configuration is never touched.
.SS "Finding and naming things"
.TP
.B udevmon scan \fR[\fB\-\-present\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-prompt\fR] [\fB\-\-all\-props\fR]
Watch events and offer to name what you see.
Plug something in (or unplug it, or mount a filesystem).
After a short pause udevmon lists what it saw and asks:
.IP
.nf
Number(s) to name, iN to ignore, Enter = scan on, q = quit:
.fi
.IP
Answer with numbers (\fB1,2\fR) to name several at once.
For each one udevmon first asks whether to register it as a
.B device
(one particular, trusted thing) or a
.B category
(a declared tag with a rule, matching every thing like it); a thing with no
serial number defaults to category, since no device match can single it out
anyway.
Then it asks for a name, and the properties to match on (Enter accepts the
suggestion and tells you how strong the match is).
For a thing with no serial number nothing is suggested: it explains that
nothing identifies the unit and asks you to choose between this model in this
port only and this model in any port (see
.BR udevmon.toml (5)).
A device is then asked for tags, comma\-separated; a category has none of its
own.
.IP
Before anything is written it shows a summary (name, match or rule with its
strength, and tags for a device) and asks:
.B [Enter]
to save (a match or rule that is not strong needs
.B y
instead; blank prints a reminder and asks again \(em a category has no such
gate),
.B r
to rename,
.B m
to redo the match or rule,
.B t
to redo the tags (device only), or
.B c
(also
.BR n ", " no " or " q )
to cancel.
Redoing a step keeps what you already answered for the others, and Enter at
that step keeps its previous value; a name already used is reported so you can
rename and try again.
Answer
.B i3
(or
.BR i1,3 )
to save things as ignored devices instead: this skips the device/category
choice and asks for no tags.
Press Ctrl+C to leave; the answer
.B q
at the number prompt cancels the batch, not just one thing.
.IP
A physical stick usually shows up twice, as a USB device and as one or more
filesystems.
Name the USB device to react to it being plugged in, and the filesystem to
react to it being mounted.
An action on a category needs
.B allow_unnamed = true
to run for things only matched that way (see
.BR udevmon.toml (5));
the command
.B scan
suggests after saving one includes it.
.IP
.B \-\-present
also offers devices and filesystems that are already present.
.B \-\-no\-prompt
only prints the events.
.B \-\-all\-props
prints every property of each thing, one per line, including the raw
.B udev_*
ones (the names usable in a rule or as
.BI { udev_NAME }
in a command); those that change on every plug and cannot be used in tag rules are
marked.
.TP
.B udevmon present \fR[\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-all\-props\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
List the USB devices and filesystems that are present now, with their
properties, how strongly each could be matched (strong, medium or weak; a device with
no serial number is marked as matching the port), whether it is named, and which tags
it has.
System filesystems are left out unless
.B \-\-all
is given.
.B \-\-all\-props
prints every property of each entry instead of the summary line, as for
.BR scan .
.B \-\-json
prints, for scripts, a JSON list with one object per entry:
.B kind
.RB ( usb " or " filesystem ),
.BR description ,
.B props
(every property, the
.B udev_*
ones included),
.B devices
and
.B tags
(the names it has in the configuration) and
.B suggested_match
(the keys
.B scan
would offer).
.TP
.B udevmon explain \fR[\fIEVENT\fR] [\fB\-\-device \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-prop \fIKEY=VALUE\fR ...] [\fB\-\-all\fR]
Show what would run, without plugging anything in.
It applies the same rules as the daemon (named or unnamed, tags,
.BR allow_unnamed ,
status, pause) and prints each command with its values filled in.
For an action that would not run it says why.
.IP
With no options it examines everything present now.
.I EVENT
limits the output to one event.
.B \-\-device
simulates that named device;
.B \-\-prop
adds a property to the simulated thing and can be repeated, which lets you try
an unnamed drive against a tag rule without owning one.
Only the properties you give are known.
Things that match no device and no tag are left out (a count says how many);
.B \-\-all
lists them and system filesystems too.
.TP
.B udevmon check
Validate the configuration file.
It prints
.B error:
lines and exits with status 1 for a file that cannot be used, and otherwise
prints
.B warning:
lines (weak matches, a tag nothing uses, a program that is not found in
.B PATH
or not executable, a hardware\-supplied value in an
.B allow_unnamed
action), then
.B note:
lines, and ends with
.BR "OK: N device(s), N tag rule(s), N action(s)" .
.SS "Devices"
.TP
.B udevmon devices list
List the named devices, with strength, match, tags, status and the number of
actions that use each.
.TP
.B udevmon devices show \fINAME\fR
Show one device: match, strength, status, tags and its actions.
.TP
.B udevmon devices add \fINAME\fR (\fB\-\-match \fIKEY=VALUE\fR ... | \fB\-\-from\-mountpoint \fIPATH\fR) [\fB\-\-tag \fITAG\fR ...] [\fB\-\-exclude\-tag \fITAG\fR ...] [\fB\-\-var \fINAME=VALUE\fR ...] [\fB\-\-description \fITEXT\fR]
Add a device without scanning.
.B \-\-match
is required and can be repeated for several keys; a device match is exact, so
repeating a key is an error.
.B \-\-from\-mountpoint
instead takes the match from the filesystem mounted at
.IR PATH :
its
.B fs_uuid
and its drive's
.BR drive_serial ,
and its description unless
.B \-\-description
is given.
Nothing is written when nothing is mounted there, the filesystem has no UUID,
the drive has no usable serial number, or a device already matches that
filesystem.
.B \-\-tag
sets an explicit tag (repeatable).
.B \-\-exclude\-tag
makes the device opt out of a rule tag (see
.BR "devices exclude" ).
.B \-\-var
sets a variable that actions can use as
.BI { var_NAME }
(repeatable).
It prints a warning if the match is weak.
.TP
.B udevmon devices remove \fINAME\fR
Remove a device.
Refused while an action names it.
.TP
.B udevmon devices tag \fINAME TAG\fR...
Add explicit tags to a device.
.TP
.B udevmon devices untag \fINAME TAG\fR...
Remove explicit tags from a device.
.TP
.B udevmon devices exclude \fINAME TAG\fR...
Make the device opt out of rule tags: the rule no longer selects it for those
tags, so their actions do not run for it.
The device stays named and keeps its own actions.
A tag the device carries explicitly, or that another matching device carries, is
not affected; carrying and excluding the same tag is an error.
.TP
.B udevmon devices include \fINAME TAG\fR...
Stop opting the device out of those tags.
.TP
.B udevmon devices set \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-match \fIKEY=VALUE\fR ...] [\fB\-\-description \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-\-var \fINAME=VALUE\fR ...] [\fB\-\-unset\-var \fINAME\fR ...]
Replace the device's whole match, and/or its description (an empty text
removes it).
.B \-\-var
sets or changes a variable and leaves the others alone (repeatable);
.B \-\-unset\-var
removes one.
Removing the only definition of a variable that an action uses is refused.
A comment after the old
.B match
value is kept.
.TP
.B udevmon devices rename \fIOLD NEW\fR
Rename a device everywhere it is used (see
.BR RENAMING ).
.TP
.B udevmon devices disable \fINAME\fR...
Turn off the device's own actions (the ones that name it).
Actions that reach it through a tag still run.
.TP
.B udevmon devices ignore \fINAME\fR...
Run nothing at all for the device, however an action would reach it.
.TP
.B udevmon devices enable \fINAME\fR...
Back to normal: clears
.B disabled
or
.BR ignored .
.PP
.BR disable ,
.B ignore
and
.B enable
take several names and change nothing unless all of them exist.
A disabled or ignored device still counts as named, so it never falls back into
the unnamed category.
.SS "Tags"
.TP
.B udevmon tags list
List the tags: declared rules and tags set on devices, with their members and
the number of actions that use each.
.TP
.B udevmon tags show \fINAME\fR
Show what a tag contains and which actions use it.
.TP
.B udevmon tags add \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-match \fIKEY=VALUE\fR ...] [\fB\-\-description \fITEXT\fR]
Declare a tag, optionally with a rule.
Repeating a key builds a list meaning \(lqany of\(rq:
.BR "\-\-match fs_type=ext4 \-\-match fs_type=exfat" .
Rules cannot use properties that change from plug to plug
.RB ( mountpoint ,
.BR devnode ,
.BR syspath ,
.B surprise
and a few raw udev ones).
In a rule
.B *
matches any run of characters and
.B ?
exactly one, as in
.BR "\-\-match fs_label=BACKUP*" ;
such a rule is rated weak.
.TP
.B udevmon tags set \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-match \fIKEY=VALUE\fR ... | \fB\-\-no\-rule\fR] [\fB\-\-description \fITEXT\fR]
Change a declared tag (one with a
.B [tags.NAME]
table).
.B \-\-match
replaces the whole rule (repeat a key to allow several values, as in
.BR "tags add" ),
and
.B \-\-no\-rule
removes it, leaving a tag that only the devices that carry it belong to.
.B \-\-description
changes the description (an empty text removes it).
A comment after the old
.B match
is kept, and a rule that uses a property that changes from plug to plug is
refused.
A tag that only devices carry has no table to change; declare it with
.BR "tags add" .
A new rule changes which things the tag's actions run for, so check the result
with
.BR "udevmon explain" .
.TP
.B udevmon tags remove \fINAME\fR
Remove a declared tag.
Refused while an action uses it.
.TP
.B udevmon tags rename \fIOLD NEW\fR
Rename a tag wherever it is used.
.SS "Actions"
.TP
.B udevmon actions list
List the actions with their targets, events and command, in the order they run
within an event (by
.BR order ,
then file order; an order other than 50 is shown).
.TP
.B udevmon actions add (\-\-device \fINAME\fR ... | \-\-tag \fINAME\fR ...) \fIEVENT\fR[,\fIEVENT\fR...] [\fB\-\-name \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout \fISECONDS\fR] [\fB\-\-all\-tags\fR] [\fB\-\-detach\fR] [\fB\-\-lock \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-retries \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-retry\-delay \fISECONDS\fR] [\fB\-\-order \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-notify\-on\-failure\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-unnamed\fR | \fB\-\-named\-only\fR] \-\- \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARG\fR...]
Add an action.
Options go before the
.BR \-\- ;
everything after it is the command, verbatim.
.B \-\-device
and
.B \-\-tag
can be repeated, and an action has devices or tags, never both.
With several tags the action runs when the thing has any of them;
.B \-\-all\-tags
requires all of them.
.I EVENT
is one or more of
.BR connected ,
.BR disconnected ,
.BR mounted ,
.BR unmounted ,
.BR removed ,
.B startup
and
.BR shutdown ,
separated by commas.
The action is added at the end of the file.
The timeout defaults to 60 seconds.
.B \-\-detach
starts the command and does not wait for it (no timeout, so it cannot be combined
with
.BR \-\-timeout ):
use it when the command is itself a long-running program.
.B \-\-lock
gives the action a lock name: actions that share one never run at the same time and
run in the order their events arrived.
.B \-\-retries
runs the command again after a non-zero exit, up to
.I N
more times, waiting
.B \-\-retry\-delay
seconds (default 1) between attempts; never after a timeout (see
.BR udevmon.toml (5)).
.B \-\-order
sets the action's
.BR order :
within one event, lower runs first (default 50; equal ones run in file order).
.B \-\-notify\-on\-failure
sets
.BR notify_on_failure :
a desktop notification when the action fails or times out.
.B \-\-allow\-unnamed
and
.B \-\-named\-only
(only with
.BR \-\-tag )
choose whether the action also runs for things that match a tag rule but that
you never named.
Without a name, the action is called after its first target and its position
in the file (for example
.BR backup\-disk\-2 ).
.TP
.B udevmon actions remove \fINAME\fR
Remove an action.
An action created by hand without a
.B name
line cannot be removed this way; edit the file.
.TP
.B udevmon actions set \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-allow\-unnamed\fR | \fB\-\-named\-only\fR | \fB\-\-default\-unnamed\fR] [\fB\-\-on \fIEVENTS\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout \fISECONDS\fR] [\fB\-\-add\-device \fINAME\fR ...] [\fB\-\-remove\-device \fINAME\fR ...] [\fB\-\-add\-tag \fINAME\fR ...] [\fB\-\-remove\-tag \fINAME\fR ...] [\fB\-\-all\-tags\fR | \fB\-\-any\-tags\fR] [\fB\-\-detach\fR | \fB\-\-no\-detach\fR] [\fB\-\-lock \fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-no\-lock\fR] [\fB\-\-retries \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-retry\-delay \fISECONDS\fR] [\fB\-\-order \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-notify\-on\-failure\fR | \fB\-\-no\-notify\-on\-failure\fR] [\fB\-\- \fICOMMAND\fR ...]
Change an action without removing and adding it again.
.B \-\-on
replaces the events (comma\-separated).
.B \-\-default\-unnamed
removes the
.B allow_unnamed
line, so the action behaves as named\-only and
.B check
notes it again if its tag has a rule.
The three
.B unnamed
options apply only to actions that use a tag.
.IP
.BR \-\-add\-device ,
.BR \-\-remove\-device ,
.B \-\-add\-tag
and
.B \-\-remove\-tag
change what the action targets.
They can be repeated and combined with the other options.
An action targets devices or tags, never both, so the device options work on
actions that use
.B device
and the tag options on actions that use
.BR tag .
A device that is added must exist; a tag needs no declaration.
Removing something the action does not target is an error, and so is removing
its last target (use
.B actions remove
for that).
Adding what is already there changes nothing.
A single name stays a plain string, a list is edited in place, and comments and
layout are kept.
.IP
.B \-\-all\-tags
makes an action with several tags run only for things that have all of them, and
.B \-\-any\-tags
goes back to the default of any of them.
They apply only to actions that use tags.
.IP
.B \\-\\-detach
makes the action start its command and not wait for it, and
.B \\-\\-no\\-detach
goes back to waiting (see
.BR udevmon.toml (5)).
An action with an explicit timeout cannot be detached.
.B \-\-lock
sets the action's lock name and
.B \-\-no\-lock
removes it.
.B \-\-retries
and
.B \-\-retry\-delay
change the retries (\fB\-\-retries 0\fR removes them).
.B \-\-order
changes the order
.RB ( "\-\-order 50" ,
the default, removes the key).
.B \-\-notify\-on\-failure
and
.B \-\-no\-notify\-on\-failure
turn the failure notification on and off.
.IP
A command after
.B \-\-
(it must come last) replaces the action's command, exactly as
.B actions add
takes it.
The new command is validated like any other, so a placeholder that the action's
events do not provide is refused and nothing is written.
A comment after the old
.B run
line is kept.
.TP
.B udevmon actions disable \fINAME\fR...
Stop actions from running without deleting them
.RB ( "enabled = false" ).
.TP
.B udevmon actions enable \fINAME\fR...
Let disabled actions run again.
.TP
.B udevmon actions rename \fIOLD NEW\fR
Give an action a new name.
.SS "Pausing"
.TP
.B udevmon pause \fR[\fB\-\-for \fIDURATION\fR]
Stop every action without touching the configuration.
.I DURATION
is a number followed by a unit, or several of them:
.BR 90s ,
.BR 30m ,
.BR 2h ,
.BR 1d ,
.BR 1h30m .
Without it the pause lasts until
.BR "udevmon resume" .
While paused nothing runs, not even
.B startup
and
.B shutdown
actions; events during the pause are logged but not replayed afterwards.
It works when no daemon is running, and applies once one starts.
See
.B FILES
for where the pause is kept.
.TP
.B udevmon resume
Let actions run again.
.SS "Shell completion"
.TP
.B udevmon completion bash
Print the bash completion script.
For a one\-off use:
.BR "source <(udevmon completion bash)" .
.TP
.B udevmon completion install \fR[\fB\-\-dest \fIFILE\fR]
Write the script to
.I ~/.local/share/bash\-completion/completions/udevmon
(or under
.BR $XDG_DATA_HOME ).
Open a new terminal to use it.
The location is loaded automatically when the
.B bash\-completion
package is installed.
.TP
.B udevmon completion uninstall \fR[\fB\-\-dest \fIFILE\fR]
Remove the installed script, if it is udevmon's own.
.PP
Completion knows the commands, options and (from your configuration) device,
tag and action names, event lists and
.B \-\-match
keys.
It reads the file given with
.B \-\-config
if there is one, and a missing or unsafe file just means no names.
Each TAB runs udevmon once and does not touch udev or D\-Bus.
.SH EDITING COMMANDS
The commands that change the configuration (the
.BR devices ,
.B tags
and
.B actions
sub\-commands other than
.B list
and
.BR show ,
and
.B scan
when it saves a device) share these behaviours:
.IP \(bu 3
Existing entries are edited with the
.B tomlkit
library, so comments, layout and order are kept, and CRLF line endings stay.
New entries are appended as text.
The previous version of the file is saved as
.IR config.toml.bak .
.IP \(bu 3
A running daemon that uses the same configuration file is told to reload, and
the command prints
.BR "Reloaded the running daemon." .
A daemon that uses another file is left alone.
.B \-\-no\-reload
skips this.
Commands that only add or remove entries do not need
.BR tomlkit ;
commands that change an existing entry do, and say how to install it if it is
missing.
.IP \(bu 3
An entry is changed, renamed or removed in the file that defines it, the main
file or a drop\-in
.RI ( config.d/NAME.toml ,
see
.BR udevmon.toml (5)).
A rename also changes the references to the old name in every file.
.BR "devices add" ,
.B tags add
and
.B actions add
write to the main file;
.BI \-\-file " DROPIN"
writes to that drop\-in instead, created if needed, given as
.IR NAME ,
.I NAME.toml
or
.IR config.d/NAME.toml .
.IP \(bu 3
They say when automation is paused.
.IP \(bu 3
If the result cannot be written correctly the file is left as it was, and the
message names what it could not find.
.SS RENAMING
.BR "devices rename" ,
.B tags rename
and
.B actions rename
change the name everywhere it is used: the table header and its sub\-tables,
the
.B device
or
.B tag
of every action, and the
.B tags
of devices.
Comments and layout are kept.
Before writing, udevmon checks that the result is exactly the old
configuration with only that name changed, in the same order; if not, nothing is
written.
The command reports what it updated, actions whose made\-up name changed, and
the comments, descriptions and commands that still mention the old name, which
it leaves alone.
A device written with dotted keys under
.B [devices]
instead of its own table cannot be renamed, and an action without a
.B name
line has to be given one first.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the configuration (what
.B udevmon reload
sends).
If the new file is invalid, the error is logged and the daemon keeps running
with the old configuration.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop normally: run the
.B shutdown
actions, wait for running actions to finish (up to their timeouts) and exit.
.PP
Shutdown actions do not run after
.B SIGKILL
or a crash, or if the machine loses power.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/udevmon/config.toml
The configuration.
The directory is
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/udevmon
if that variable is set.
The file must be owned by you and not writable by group or others; udevmon
refuses to load it otherwise (\c
.BR "chmod go\-w" ).
.TP
.I ~/.config/udevmon/config.d/*.toml
Drop\-in files, read after the main one in name order.
.TP
.I ~/.config/udevmon/config.toml.bak
The previous version, written by every editing command (a drop\-in gets its own
.IR NAME.toml.bak ).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/udevmon.lock
Held by the running daemon.
Line one is its process ID, line two the resolved path of its configuration.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/udevmon.sock
The running daemon's control socket, used by
.BR "udevmon trigger" .
Only you can connect to it.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/udevmon.paused
Exists while automation is paused.
It is empty (until resumed) or holds the time the pause ends.
It survives a daemon restart but not a logout or reboot.
If
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is not set, these files are in
.IR /tmp/udevmon\-UID .
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/udevmon/state/
The per\-instance folders that actions can leave notes in (private, removed when the
plug or mount ends, wiped when the daemon starts and stops).
See
.BR udevmon.toml (5).
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/udevmon.service
The unit written by
.BR "service install" .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/bash\-completion/completions/udevmon
The completion script written by
.BR "completion install" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base of the configuration and systemd unit directories.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base of the completion script directory.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the lock and pause files are kept.
.PP
Commands started by actions receive your environment plus
.BR UDEVMON_EVENT ,
.B UDEVMON_DEVICE
(when the thing is named),
.B UDEVMON_TAG
(tag actions), one
.BI UDEVMON_VAR_ NAME
per variable of the device,
.B UDEVMON_STATE_DIR
(the private folder of the plug or mount), and one
.BI UDEVMON_ PROPERTY
per property, for example
.B UDEVMON_MOUNTPOINT
and
.BR UDEVMON_SURPRISE .
Under the service, the graphical session's
.B DISPLAY
and related variables are looked up for every action, so graphical programs
work whether the service started before or after you logged in.
See
.BR udevmon.toml (5).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error: an invalid or unsafe configuration, a name that does not exist, an
edit that could not be made,
.B reload
with no daemon running, and so on.
The message goes to standard error (for
.BR check ,
to standard output).
.SH EXAMPLES
Name a stick and react to it being mounted:
.PP
.nf
udevmon scan
udevmon actions add \-\-device backup\-disk mounted \e
  \-\- notify\-send "Backup disk ready" "{mountpoint}"
udevmon \-v run
.fi
.PP
Run in the background:
.PP
.nf
udevmon service install \-\-now
journalctl \-\-user \-u udevmon \-f
.fi
.PP
Warn when a drive is pulled out without being ejected:
.PP
.nf
udevmon actions add \-\-device backup\-disk removed \e
  \-\- notify\-send \-u critical "Pulled without ejecting" "{fs_label}"
.fi
.PP
One action for every ext4 or exfat drive, and a backup only for the ones you
named and tagged:
.PP
.nf
udevmon tags add storage \-\-match fs_type=ext4 \-\-match fs_type=exfat
udevmon actions add \-\-tag storage mounted \-\-allow\-unnamed \e
  \-\- notify\-send "Drive mounted: {tag}"
udevmon devices tag backup\-disk backup
udevmon actions add \-\-tag backup mounted \-\-named\-only \e
  \-\- ~/bin/backup.sh "{mountpoint}"
.fi
.PP
See what would run for a simulated unnamed drive:
.PP
.nf
udevmon explain mounted \-\-prop fs_type=ext4 \e
  \-\-prop mountpoint=/media/x
.fi
.PP
Stop everything for half an hour:
.PP
.nf
udevmon pause \-\-for 30m
.fi
.SH SECURITY
Running commands when hardware appears is convenient and a classic attack
vector.
udevmon never passes a command through a shell and passes each value as one
argument, so a hostile label cannot break out of the command line.
It refuses a configuration that is not yours or is writable by others, warns
about weak matches, and runs actions for things you have not named only when the
action sets
.BR "allow_unnamed = true" .
Prefer serial numbers and UUIDs, which anyone can imitate less easily than a
vendor and product ID, and keep
.B allow_unnamed
actions harmless.
See
.BR udevmon.toml (5).
.SH LIMITATIONS
Events are seen only while your session is running and udevmon is started, and
missed events are not replayed (apart from
.BR startup ).
Filesystem events come from udisks2, so mounts done with
.B sudo mount
are not seen.
Only USB devices and udisks2 filesystems are supported out of the box; other
udev filters are experimental.
Rule values are strings, lists of them, or patterns with
.B *
and
.BR ? ;
there are no regular expressions.
Variables belong to devices, not to tags.
.SH BUGS
None known beyond the limitations above.
